After a quarter century of near-zero yields, Japan’s 20-year government bond is trading close to 3%, levels last seen in the late 1990s. Recent data show the 20-year yield around 2.9–2.94%, while the 30-year has climbed above 3.4%, both multi-decade highs driven by expectations that the Bank of Japan (BoJ) will keep raising rates as inflation stays above target. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0} The shift follows the BoJ’s March 2024 decision to end its yield-curve-control (YCC) regime and negative-rate framework, moving toward a more conventional policy setup. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

Because Japan has long been the world’s largest provider of cheap funding, higher domestic yields have global consequences. Japanese institutions such as GPIF, banks and insurers collectively hold over $1.1 trillion in US Treasuries and large portfolios of overseas assets. :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2} As returns at home improve, the incentive to repatriate capital rises, potentially putting upward pressure on US and European yields and tightening liquidity for risk assets, including crypto. Recent sell-offs in global bonds and a sharp drop in Bitcoin prices after hawkish BoJ comments illustrate how sensitive markets already are to Japan’s normalization path. :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

This article explains how Japan reached this turning point, why the end of "free money" in yen matters for global markets, and how different yield scenarios could shape the risk environment for Bitcoin and other digital assets over the next cycle.
